course: dictionary
lesson: 628
title: Babysit and the words around it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To babysit means to look after a baby or a child for a while when their parents are out. It is a verb, so you can say I babysit, she babysits, they babysat, and we are babysitting.
explain: Read the context: «You can swap time and help as well as things, like neighbours who babysit for each other.» Neighbours help each other, so one looks after the other's children and the favour is returned. Try your meaning in the sentence: looking after children for each other still makes sense.
explain: The word can also be used as a joke. If someone says they are babysitting a goldfish while the family is away, the clue is that a fish is not a baby. So the meaning is looking after something for a while.
fact: «Babysit» means to look after a child for a while when their parents are out.
fact: Babysitting is often done in the evening by teenagers, grandparents or friends.
fact: A good babysitter knows the emergency numbers and listens to the child.
fact: Jokingly, you can say you are babysitting a pet or a house when you watch over it.
fact: The past tense is «babysat», as in «She babysat her little brother last night.»
exercise: «You can swap time and help as well as things, like neighbours who babysit for each other.» What does babysit mean here? | To sell toys in a shop | *To look after children while their parents are out | To sleep over at a friend's house | To cook dinner for the whole street
exercise: Ella says, «I babysat my little brother last night.» What did Ella do? | She bought him a new bike | She played football at the park | She went to bed early | *She looked after her little brother while their parents were out
exercise: Which sentence uses babysit as a joke? | Ravi says he is reading a book to his baby sister | *Ravi says he is babysitting his goldfish while the family is away | Ravi says he is making lunch for his dad
test: Why would a family want a babysitter to know the emergency numbers? | *To keep the child safe if something goes wrong | To watch the football | To learn new songs | To pay for the dinner
test: Which word is the past tense of babysit? | babysits | *babysat | babysitting | babysitter
exam: Which phrase best explains babysit in «neighbours who babysit for each other»? | Race each other to the park | Buy each other sweets | *Look after each other's children | Sing to each other at bedtime
